  "comments": [
    {
      "paragraph_index": 2,
      "comment": "Dissolve sodium bicarbonate in deionized water (100 mM Na2HCO3)."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 3,
      "comment": "Bubble solution with an oxygen-free gas mixture of N2-CO2 (70:30)."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 5,
      "comment": "While gassing, add 10 ml of Solutions A (see below) and 2 ml of Solution B (see below)."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 6,
      "comment": "The high initial pH of this solution may cause the minerals to precipitate. As the pH decreases as a result of equilibration of the CO2: bicarbonate buffering system, the minerals will go back into solution; at the same time O2 is driven out of the medium."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 7,
      "comment": ""
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 8,
      "comment": "Next, add and dissolve dry ingredients in the order listed:"
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 10,
      "comment": "The medium is allowed to gas until it has become clear, usually 30 to 60 minutes depending on the gas flow rate."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 11,
      "comment": "The pH of the clear medium should be between pH 7 and pH 7.3."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 12,
      "comment": "At this point add 2 ml of Solution C (see below) and 10 ml of Trace Minerals Supplement (available from ATCC, cat. MD-TMS) to the medium."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 14,
      "comment": "The medium is then ready to be transferred to culture tubes."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 15,
      "comment": "Dispense with continual gassing. After the all tubes are filled, capped, and crimped, autoclave the medium at 121ºC."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 16,
      "comment": "Following autoclaving a precipitate may form; with periodic mixing this should go back into solution within 48 hours."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 18,
      "comment": ""
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 20,
      "comment": "(100X solution; use 10 ml of solution A per liter of medium)"
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 21,
      "comment": "Dissolve in deionized water, adjust pH to 4.0 with HCl and bring up to one liter."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 22,
      "comment": ""
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 24,
      "comment": "(500X solution; use 2 ml of solution B per liter of medium)"
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 25,
      "comment": "Dissolve in deionized water then bring the volume up to one liter."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 26,
      "comment": ""
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 28,
      "comment": "(500X; use 2 ml of solution C per liter of medium)"
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 29,
      "comment": "Dissolve in deionized water, and then bring the volume up to one liter."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 30,
      "comment": ""
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 32,
      "comment": ""
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 33,
      "comment": "Reducing Agents"
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 34,
      "comment": "We suggest adding the reducing agent to the medium at least one hour before the medium is to be inoculated."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 35,
      "comment": ""
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 37,
      "comment": "Distribute into screw cap test tubes, 5–6 ml per tube and seal with rubber stoppers under N2 gas."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 38,
      "comment": "Autoclave at 121ºC to sterilize. Excess tubes can be stored at room temperature for up to 2 months."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 39,
      "comment": "Co-enzyme M is a compound produced by many methanogens. Some methanogens are sensitive to stronger reducing agents such as sodium sulfide. Co-enzyme M is the standard reducing agent we use when working with methanogens."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 40,
      "comment": ""
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 42,
      "comment": "Distribute into screw cap test tubes, 5–6 ml per tube, and seal with Hungate stoppers. Autoclave at 121ºC to sterilize. Excess tubes can be stored frozen for up to 6 months."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 43,
      "comment": "Once thawed a tube of sodium sulfide should not be used for more than a week."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 44,
      "comment": "CAUTON: if sodium sulfide comes into contact with a strong acid, hydrogen sulfide (H2S), a very toxic gas is liberated immediately."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 45,
      "comment": ""
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 47,
      "comment": "Distribute into screw cap test tubes, 5–6 ml per tube, and seal with Hungate stoppers. Autoclave at 121ºC to sterilize."
    },
    {
      "paragraph_index": 48,
      "comment": "Excess tubes can be stored frozen for up to 6 months. Once thawed, a tube of cysteine should not be used for more than a week."
    }
  ]
}
